  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 300 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA large area of surface high pressure will settle over much of \nthe eastern CONUS Sunday night into Monday. The associated surface\nridge will nose westward into our forecast area through Monday \nafternoon. A solid deck of low stratus may hold strong across much\nof the region, especially along and east of the mountains. High \ntemperatures on Monday still look to struggle to reach the lower \nto mid 50s over most locations, except for slightly warmer \nreadings in the 60s along the Rio Grande. Another mostly cloudy \nand cool days with temperatures in the 50s and 60s is forecast on \nTuesday, with the coolest readings over our eastern areas where \nclouds may persist through the day. Southwesterly flow aloft \nincreases late Tuesday into Wednesday ahead of the next system \nlifting over the Four Corners. Associated moisture will stay well \nto our northwest with this feature, so dry conditions will \ncontinue through midweek. Temperatures should trend slightly \nwarmer in the 60s for most places by Wednesday. Southwesterly flow\naloft will generally persist over our forecast area through late \nnext week with generally southerly surface flow. Increased \nmoisture and shortwave impulses embedded in the southwesterly mid \nlevel flow may bring another low chance (20-30%) of rain to our \neastern and northern areas late Thursday through late Friday. \nHighs otherwise look to trend slightly warmer in the upper 60s to \nmid 70s by Friday afternoon for most locations. Morning lows \nshould continue to range in the 40s and 50s.",
